<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Related OCP Academy training</title><style>@font-face{font-family:Lato;src:url(lato2-regular.woff)}@font-face{font-family:Lato;src:url(lato2-bold.woff);font-weight:700}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{font:18px/1.6 Lato,Arial,sans-serif;color:#34383c;background:#f3f5f8;margin:0}header{background:#24265f;color:white;padding:32px max(24px,calc((100vw - 1000px)/2))}header img{width:150px;height:60px;object-fit:contain;object-position:left}h1{font-size:36px;line-height:1.18;margin:20px 0 14px}h2{font-size:25px;color:#343895;line-height:1.3}h3{font-size:20px}main{max-width:1050px;margin:36px auto;padding:0 24px 50px}article,section.card{background:white;border:1px solid #dbe1e8;border-radius:14px;padding:28px;margin:24px 0}a{color:#343895;text-underline-offset:3px}a:hover{color:#527b20}p{margin:12px 0}nav{display:flex;gap:15px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:18px 0}.badge{background:#8DC63F;color:#192048;border-radius:50px;padding:5px 12px;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;display:inline-block}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin:20px 0}th,td{padding:12px;border:1px solid #cbd1d9;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}th{background:#eef2f7}li{margin:9px 0}.answer{min-height:95px;border:1px solid #aeb9c6;border-radius:5px;padding:12px;white-space:pre-wrap}.muted{color:#69727d;font-size:15px}button,.button{background:#343895;color:white;border:0;border-radius:7px;padding:10px 17px;font:inherit;text-decoration:none;cursor:pointer}button:focus-visible,a:focus-visible,[contenteditable]:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #8DC63F;outline-offset:4px}.compact{font-size:16px}@media(max-width:600px){body{font-size:16px}h1{font-size:29px}article,section.card{padding:20px}th,td{padding:8px;font-size:14px}table{display:block;overflow:auto}}@media print{header{background:white;color:#343895;padding:0}header img,nav,button{display:none}body{background:white;font-size:11pt}main{max-width:none;margin:0;padding:0}article,section.card{break-inside:avoid;border:0;padding:0;margin:22px 0}.answer{min-height:90px}h1{font-size:22pt}}</style></head><body><header><img src="../ocp_academy_white.svg" alt="OCP Academy"><h1>Related OCP Academy training</h1><p>Six companion courses for deeper technical and deployment context</p></header><main><p>Choose a learning path that develops the question you are working on. These courses are optional related learning; they do not add completion requirements to the LVDC course.</p><article><span class="badge">C1</span><h2><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://academy.opencompute.org/learn/courses/76/grid-disturbance-performance-for-hyperscale-data-centers">Grid Disturbance Performance for Hyperscale Data Centers</a></h2><p>Utility-interface scope, voltage/time ride-through envelopes, active-power recovery, cooling coordination and implementation choices (four modules).</p><p><strong>Why it helps:</strong> Develop the grid-side obligations without treating local DC continuity as proof of utility compliance.</p><p class="muted">Used in this course: M5 S3 utility interface; M7 S5 cooling-aware recovery.</p></article><article><span class="badge">C2</span><h2><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://academy.opencompute.org/learn/courses/69/open-data-center-for-ai-applying-the-specification">Open Data Center for AI: Applying Specification 0.7</a></h2><p>Facility compatibility, moving loads and clearances, hall/row power and cooling capacity, and evidence for site variations (four modules).</p><p><strong>Why it helps:</strong> Connect LVDC decisions to the wider facility envelope and a documented compatibility review.</p><p class="muted">Used in this course: M1 S7 architecture requirements; M10 S6 unresolved interface evidence.</p></article><article><span class="badge">C3</span><h2><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://academy.opencompute.org/learn/courses/59/ocp-solid-state-transformers-from-medium-voltage-to-800-vdc">OCP Solid State Transformers: From Medium Voltage to 800 VDC</a></h2><p>SST conversion stages, the OCP v0.3 product envelope, grounding, grid behavior, control states and SST/ESS integration (five modules).</p><p><strong>Why it helps:</strong> Provide product and technology depth beyond this course’s system architecture treatment.</p><p class="muted">Used in this course: M1 S5 conversion and isolation; M5 S4 converter functions.</p></article><article><span class="badge">C4</span><h2><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://academy.opencompute.org/learn/courses/50/ocp-ready-requirements-for-energy-storage-systems">OCP Ready™ Requirements for Energy Storage Systems</a></h2><p>ESS placement, coupling and controls; electrical, thermal and fire hazards; testing, operating response, codes and OCP Ready™ evidence (four modules).</p><p><strong>Why it helps:</strong> Extend the storage discussion into deployment, hazard mitigation and acceptance evidence.</p><p class="muted">Used in this course: M5 S5 storage placement; M6 S7 rack buffering; M9 S7 verification evidence.</p></article><article><span class="badge">C5</span><h2><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://academy.opencompute.org/learn/courses/49/ocp-diablo-400">OCP Diablo 400</a></h2><p>Diablo v0.7.0 sidecar architecture, AC input/DC output, buffering, grounding, interfaces and telemetry (core modules 1–3).</p><p><strong>Why it helps:</strong> Show the rack-side product boundary. Its optional facility-LVDC module is earlier context, not the v1.1 baseline.</p><p class="muted">Used in this course: M6 S3 rack interface approaches, alongside the sidecar comparison.</p></article><article><span class="badge">C6</span><h2><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://academy.opencompute.org/learn/courses/48/ocp-project-deschutes-coolant-distribution-unit-cdu-v10">OCP Project Deschutes: Coolant Distribution Unit (CDU) v1.0</a></h2><p>CDU architecture, pumps and heat exchangers, control loops, redundant power feeds, deployment and serviceability (three modules).</p><p><strong>Why it helps:</strong> Connect the cooling-dependency discussion to a concrete CDU and its electrical, control and service interfaces.</p><p class="muted">Used in this course: M5 S7 coolant distribution units, cooling dependencies and monitoring.</p></article><section class="card"><h2>Keep the baseline clear</h2><p>The LVDC white paper v1.1.0 governs this course. Earlier facility-LVDC material in the SST and Diablo courses provides context and does not supersede this baseline.</p><nav><a href="https://www.opencompute.org/documents/dcf-power-distribution-lvdc-white-paper-version-1-1-0-pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official LVDC paper</a><a href="lvdc_whitepaper_v1_1_0.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bundled paper</a><a href="review_worksheet.html">Review worksheet</a></nav></section></main></body></html>